{"subjectId":"javascript","id":"async-await","title":"async / await","group":"Asynchronous JavaScript","order":45,"html":"<p class=\"lead\"><code>async</code>/<code>await</code> is syntax over promises that lets asynchronous code read top-to-bottom like synchronous code — with ordinary <code>try/catch</code> for errors.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-list-check\"></i>The rules</h3>\n<ul><li>An <code>async</code> function <strong>always returns a promise</strong> — a returned value is wrapped, a thrown error becomes a rejection.</li><li><code>await p</code> pauses <em>that function</em> (not the thread) until <code>p</code> settles, then returns the value or throws the reason.</li><li><code>await</code> works inside <code>async</code> functions and at the top level of ES modules.</li></ul>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>async function getNumber() { return 7; }\nasync function explode() { throw new Error(\"async boom\"); }\n\nconsole.log(getNumber() instanceof Promise);\nconsole.log(await getNumber());\ntry {\n  await explode();\n} catch (e) {\n  console.log(\"caught:\", e.message);\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>true\n7\ncaught: async boom</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-transform\"></i>Rewriting a promise chain</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>const db = {\n  user: (id) =&gt; new Promise((r) =&gt; setTimeout(() =&gt; r({ id, name: \"Asha\" }), 5)),\n  orders: (user) =&gt; new Promise((r) =&gt; setTimeout(() =&gt; r([{ total: 30 }, { total: 12 }]), 5)),\n};\n\n// with then\ndb.user(1)\n  .then((user) =&gt; db.orders(user).then((orders) =&gt; ({ user, orders })))\n  .then(({ user, orders }) =&gt; console.log(\"then:\", user.name, orders.length));\n\n// with async/await\nasync function summary(id) {\n  const user = await db.user(id);\n  const orders = await db.orders(user);\n  const total = orders.reduce((s, o) =&gt; s + o.total, 0);\n  return `${user.name} spent ${total}`;\n}\nconsole.log(\"await:\", await summary(1));</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>then: Asha 2\nawait: Asha spent 42</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrows-split\"></i>Do not serialise independent work</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>const fetchA = () =&gt; new Promise((r) =&gt; setTimeout(() =&gt; r(\"A\"), 30));\nconst fetchB = () =&gt; new Promise((r) =&gt; setTimeout(() =&gt; r(\"B\"), 30));\n\nlet t = performance.now();\nconst a1 = await fetchA();\nconst b1 = await fetchB();                  // waits for A first: ~60 ms\nconst slow = performance.now() - t;\n\nt = performance.now();\nconst [a2, b2] = await Promise.all([fetchA(), fetchB()]);   // concurrently: ~30 ms\nconst fast = performance.now() - t;\nconsole.log(a1 + b1, a2 + b2, slow &gt; 55, fast &lt; 50);</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>AB AB true true</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-repeat\"></i>await in loops</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>const double = (n) =&gt; new Promise((r) =&gt; setTimeout(() =&gt; r(n * 2), 5));\nconst nums = [1, 2, 3];\n\nconst wrong = nums.forEach(async (n) =&gt; await double(n));   // forEach ignores promises\nconsole.log(\"forEach returns:\", wrong);\n\nconst out = [];\nfor (const n of nums) out.push(await double(n));            // sequential, correct\nconsole.log(out, await Promise.all(nums.map(double)));      // parallel, correct</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>forEach returns: undefined\n[ 2, 4, 6 ] [ 2, 4, 6 ]</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p><code>array.forEach(async …)</code> fires all callbacks and returns immediately — nothing waits for them and their errors are unhandled. Use <code>for…of</code> with <code>await</code> (sequential) or <code>Promise.all(arr.map(…))</code> (parallel).</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-shield\"></i>Error handling patterns</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>const risky = (ok) =&gt; (ok ? Promise.resolve(\"data\") : Promise.reject(new Error(\"network\")));\n\n// 1. try / catch / finally\nasync function load() {\n  try {\n    return await risky(false);     // `return await` so the catch applies\n  } catch (e) {\n    return `fallback (${e.message})`;\n  } finally {\n    console.log(\"hide spinner\");\n  }\n}\nconsole.log(await load());\n\n// 2. Go-style tuple helper\nconst to = (p) =&gt; p.then((v) =&gt; [null, v], (e) =&gt; [e, null]);\nconst [err, data] = await to(risky(true));\nconsole.log(err, data);</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>hide spinner\nfallback (network)\nnull data</code></pre></figure>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What does an async function return?</summary><div><p>Always a Promise. A returned value resolves it; a thrown error rejects it; a returned promise is adopted.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Does await block the thread?</summary><div><p>No. It suspends only the current async function; the rest of the function becomes a microtask continuation, and the event loop keeps running other code meanwhile.</p></div></details></section>"}